protection of electronic documents heats up, new encryption programs
may preempt the need to overhaul the current copyright law by
providing a technical fix instead. The Electronic Copyright Management
System, developed by the Corporation for National Research
Initiatives, the Library of Congress, and DOD's Advanced Research
Projects Agency, will be tested at the University of Illinois later
this month. The purchaser of digital data would receive a
cryptographic key to decode the document. A cypher would then exist in
the document, identifying the owner and the buyer. If the buyer
distributes the document on a BBS, for instance, cybercops will be
able to trace the transgression back to the offending party. Another
approach would turn a document purchased for one-time use into
gibberish when transmitted via e-mail. (Business Week 10/17/94 p.212)

