Subject: [#94-10-330] BOOK: The Bell Curve (Any thoughts?)

 davisd@nimitz.ee.washington.edu wrote: 
>
>While I consider self acceptance through self esteem superior to self
>acceptance through sharing in some predicate (Man, rational being,
>life, etc.,), I still consider it a trap. Instead of acting as the
>subject doing the valuing, one treats oneself as an object to be
>valued or disvalued. 
>
This is something I've been chewing on lately--self-valuing is
weird in that it crumbles as soon it's questioned seriously.

Part of what's gotten it onto my mind is the people who want
to argue that self-esteem must be earned and that encouraging
it in people who are doing badly is pernicious. The problem
with that belief is that, if people don't value themselves, they
have little reason to do better with their lives.

Branden distinguishes between "self-esteem" and "a primitive
sense of self-affirmation", but the later is too long a phrase
to use effectively in arguments. :-) "Primitive" might be
a little patronizing, too.

"Self-valuing" might be a useful term--"self-esteem" is an
opinion, and "self-valuing" is a loyalty.

Nancy Lebovitz

