Subject: [#94-10-334] Argument by Analogy (Was: Reply to Challenged Uploaders)

I was surprised to see Tim Starr saying that he didn't feel he had to
define consciousness before debating whether machines could have it. 
Normally, as I recall, the definition of words has been quite important
to Tim.  :-) :-)

My definition:  consciousness is the modelling of the world by a mind
which is extended to include itself, especially it's own thought
processes.  This permits introspection, so that such a conscious mind
can ask questions to itself ("ponder") about what it is thinking,
feeling etc.

Result: Apples are not conscious at all.  I am and, if you can talk
about your mental state, so are you.  When machines can discourse
likewise so also are they conscious.

